this week's podcast.

66
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:08,760
So let's get started, shall we?

67
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:54,040
My favorite all time boy scout-

68
00:04:54,040 --> 00:04:57,920
He's my favorite all time boy scout-

69
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:03,920
Send me a letter. send it by name,

70
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:06,280
E-mail, that is folks.

71
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:17,440
And here's an answer to one of your e-mails.

72
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:22,880
Bill Chapman is the Scoutmaster, Troop 736 in Orange County, California,

73
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:26,160
And he said: what are your thoughts on merit badge classes?

74
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:29,640
Frankly, Bill, I don't care for merit badge classes.

75
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:31,920
I don't care for anything in Scouts

76
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:35,320
that we're going to call a class,

77
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:39,680
Because I think that scouting, learning and education

78
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:44,040
is radically different than the standard formal type

79
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:47,400
of education our Scouts receive in school.

80
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:49,680
So when people call something and scouting a class

81
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:51,800
it kind of rubs me the wrong way.

82
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:53,800
Various influence have always been

83
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:57,360
aimed at reducing things like merit badges

84
00:05:57,360 --> 00:05:58,760
to a classroom experience,

85
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:03,080
And it's kind of inevitable because that's what we're used to,

86
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:04,840
That's what we know.

87
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:07,600
There are workbooks and worksheets and lesson plans

88
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:09,320
and all sorts of other things that I

89
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:11,840
consider to be mischief that reduce

90
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:16,000
what could be really great hands-on active learning

91
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,480
experiences to a dull slog through exercises that

92
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:24,280
sit Scouts on their backside watching PowerPoint presentations

93
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:26,800
and ticking off requirements.

94
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:31,120
Now I'm not complaining about the quality of the instruction

95
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:33,200
or the quality of the work that gets done,

96
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:36,720
But if we have things that we call classes,

97
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:39,840
I can almost guarantee we're missing an opportunity

98
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,960
to do some real scouting,

99
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:45,040
And I'm actually working on a. it

100
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:48,400
may end up being a book about scouting education

101
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:51,360
and with a lot of practical methods

102
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:55,760
to avoid bringing in the more formal kind of classroom

103
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:58,840
education methods that we typically default to,

104
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:02,200
And I'm hoping to have that done by the first part of next year.

105
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:06,000
Mike Martin is with Troop 186 and he's a scout master.

106
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,480
He said I was voted in as the new scout master

107
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:11,360
and I'm trying to tell everyone that Scouts should

108
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:13,800
be buying their own food, packing their own food,

109
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:16,040
cooking their own food and cleaning up

110
00:07:16,040 --> 00:07:17,920
from their meals as a patrol,

111
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:20,440
And this has never happened in our Troop in the past.

112
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:22,600
Well, first, Mike, good for you,

113
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:26,480
because that is exactly what ought to be happening.

114
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:29,240
I'm getting some pushback from one of my assistant scout

115
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:32,360
masters that thinks this is going to be confusing

116
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:34,440
and questions who's going to buy the food

117
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:36,520
and all those different things.

118
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:39,280
My response has been that it'll be up to the patrol leaders

119
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:41,160
to organize it and make it happen.

120
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:43,000
The trickiest part is how it's paid for.

121
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,120
I think the patrol can make their own menu,

122
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:49,880
buy their own food and then have the members of the patrol

123
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:52,120
reimburse whoever bought the food.

124
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:53,800
I've heard other troops go shopping

125
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:55,920
during the meeting times the week of the camp out,

126
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:57,600
which might be a good idea,

127
00:07:57,600 --> 00:08:01,400
but I'm asking your advice on how to make this happen.

128
00:08:01,400 --> 00:08:03,080
Well, Mike, you'll have a lot of fun

129
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:05,440
and develop some leadership skills

130
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:07,880
if you put the question of exactly how that's going

131
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:10,160
to happen directly to your patrol leaders, council

132
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:12,880
and work with them at forming a plan.

133
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:14,280
They'll get stuck here and there,

134
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:15,760
probably in this process,

135
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:18,400
So help them out. make some suggestions

136
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:20,040
Now. based on my experience,

137
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:23,840
the most practical idea is setting a budget for shopping.

138
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:28,760
For instance, we budget $10 per scout for food over a weekend

139
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:31,120
And that usually works out just fine.

140
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:34,560
If a patrol wants to do something above that $10 budget,

141
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:37,200
then they need to cost it out before they do,

142
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:40,480
And if it takes an extra five or 10 bucks per scout,

143
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:42,640
they ought to make sure that the families know

144
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:44,520
and understand that ahead of time

145
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:46,040
And that all the scouts and the patrol

146
00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:47,800
are part of making that decision

147
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:50,920
The first time out. you may consider having the patrol leaders

148
00:08:50,920 --> 00:08:52,960
be the ones who actually buy the food.

149
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:54,960
have them meet you at the grocery store

150
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:57,040
after the patrol's figured out a menu

151
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:00,360
and you can help them navigate through this the first time.

152
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:03,360
In doing that, you see, you will have trained them

153
00:09:03,360 --> 00:09:05,920
to train the other scouts and their patrol,

154
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:08,400
And the way that that happens is actually outlined

155
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:10,360
in one of the first couple of chapters

156
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:13,080
of the narrative that we've been publishing

157
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:15,560
over the past several weeks, called a new scout master.

158
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:17,120
So make sure to check that out.

159
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:18,160
Steve wrote in to say

160
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:21,880
I'm currently preparing for a University of Scouting program

161
00:09:21,880 --> 00:09:25,360
on debunking the urban legends of scouting.

162
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:27,560
An example of an urban legend would be

163
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:29,480
that scouts must wear their uniforms

164
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:32,080
while traveling to be covered by insurance.

165
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:33,480
I'd appreciate it if you could send me

166
00:09:33,480 --> 00:09:34,960
a few of your favorites.

167
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:37,600
Well, Steve, I think one very useful thing

168
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:40,360
for you to do in that program

169
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:43,720
would be to make sure that people are provided

170
00:09:43,720 --> 00:09:47,080
with the three publications that debunk

171
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:49,480
all of the urban legends of scouting.

172
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:51,120
I'm gonna direct you by a link

173
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:53,240
in the post that contains this podcast

174
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:57,840
to links of PDF versions of these three publications:

175
00:09:57,840 --> 00:09:59,680
The Guide to Safe Scouting,

176
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:02,120
the Guide to Advancement 2013,

177
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:04,720
and the Uniform and Insignia Manual.

178
00:10:04,720 --> 00:10:06,840
Now you asked for some of my favorite

179
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:07,960
scouting urban legends.

180
00:10:07,960 --> 00:10:10,920
Well, wearing the uniform seems to be

181
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:12,920
the most persistent myth,

182
00:10:12,920 --> 00:10:16,160
And many folks are surprised when they find out

183
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:19,520
that scouts are never absolutely required

184
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:20,800
to wear a uniform.

185
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:22,680
Because if you look at the Guide to Awards

186
00:10:22,680 --> 00:10:26,680
and Insignia I mentioned before, on page five

187
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:27,800
it says this:

188
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:30,800
while wearing the uniform is not mandatory,

189
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:32,560
it is highly encouraged.

190
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:33,600
The leaders of scouting,

191
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:35,080
both volunteer and professional,

192
00:10:35,080 --> 00:10:36,800
promote the wearing of the correct

193
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:39,720
complete uniform on all suitable occasions.

194
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:42,600
So wearing the uniform is not mandatory.

195
00:10:42,600 --> 00:10:45,520
It's highly encouraged, but it's not mandatory.

196
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:47,680
Now, of course, what I think really needs attention

197
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:51,640
are not so much policy myths or misunderstandings,

198
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:53,600
but basic misunderstandings

199
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:56,520
about the motivations behind scouting.

200
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:59,000
These are more attitudinal issues

201
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,920
than policy misunderstandings,

202
00:11:00,920 --> 00:11:03,120
The most common among those being

203
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:05,160
that scouts are somehow not trustworthy

204
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:07,719
and they must be policed by the establishment

205
00:11:07,719 --> 00:11:09,360
of lots of rules and regulations,

206
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:12,079
metrics, tests and proofs,

207
00:11:12,079 --> 00:11:14,599
or that scoutmasters employ youth leaders

208
00:11:14,599 --> 00:11:17,319
like their employees to carry out responsibilities

209
00:11:17,319 --> 00:11:19,560
they would otherwise have to do for themselves.

210
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:21,599
both attitudinal myths

211
00:11:21,599 --> 00:11:24,240
that often need a little bit of correction.

212
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:25,719
George wrote in to say

213
00:11:25,719 --> 00:11:28,400
our troop holds leadership elections

214
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:31,680
in what I consider to be an unusual manner.

215
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:33,120
The scouts elect a slate

216
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:35,400
of potential senior patrol leaders

217
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:37,400
and then a board made up of adults

218
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:39,720
interview those potential senior patrol leaders

219
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:43,319
and selects the quote winner, unquote.

220
00:11:43,319 --> 00:11:45,360
I've never seen this done anywhere else

221
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:46,959
and I kind of feel like it undermines

222
00:11:46,959 --> 00:11:49,280
the concept of youth run

223
00:11:49,280 --> 00:11:51,520
so far as a scout troop is concerned.

224
00:11:51,520 --> 00:11:52,560
What do you think?

225
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:55,640
Well, George, that's a new one on me,

226
00:11:55,640 --> 00:11:57,680
Of course, as you suspect,

227
00:11:57,680 --> 00:12:00,760
it undermines the choice of the scouts, doesn't it?

228
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:05,160
Either they're going to elect their leaders directly

229
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:07,320
or adults are gonna get involved

230
00:12:07,320 --> 00:12:08,920
in some kind of chicanery

231
00:12:08,920 --> 00:12:11,280
that marginalizes their choice.

232
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:14,000
It's interesting how many different ways

233
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:17,400
adults find to invalidate youth choices

234
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:20,720
or coerce the results that they would like to see.

235
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:22,600
Why even go through the artifice

236
00:12:22,600 --> 00:12:24,280
of having the youth involved at all?

237
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:26,040
Just pick the senior patrol leader.

238
00:12:26,079 --> 00:12:27,839
if that's the way you're going to do it,

239
00:12:27,839 --> 00:12:29,560
I think it's a little craven

240
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:32,000
to try and hide behind this idea

241
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:33,839
that while we're letting the scouts choose

242
00:12:33,839 --> 00:12:37,839
a slate of candidates from whom we will choose the winner,

243
00:12:37,839 --> 00:12:39,400
Come on. you know,

244
00:12:39,400 --> 00:12:40,800
you don't have to go any further

245
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:42,079
than the Scout Master Handbook

246
00:12:42,079 --> 00:12:44,439
to learn that the senior patrol leaders

247
00:12:44,439 --> 00:12:47,000
are elected by all the scouts in the troop,

248
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,120
and it's just that, plain and simple.

249
00:12:49,120 --> 00:12:50,160
Pete got in touch with me.

250
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:52,719
He said I lost two scouts in one week

251
00:12:52,719 --> 00:12:54,599
to another troop in town.

252
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:55,920
The father of the first one,

253
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:58,880
who is also one of my assistant scout masters,

254
00:12:58,880 --> 00:13:01,600
told me his son wanted to be with his friends

255
00:13:01,600 --> 00:13:03,720
who were members of the other troop.

256
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:05,200
When the first scout left,

257
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:06,840
a second one followed

258
00:13:06,840 --> 00:13:10,600
whose father happens to be another assistant scout master,

259
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:12,080
and that really hurt.

260
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:13,400
It's bothered me so much.

261
00:13:13,400 --> 00:13:14,480
I've had trouble sleeping

262
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:16,960
trying to figure out if I did anything wrong.

263
00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:20,200
I put a lot of time and effort and money into scouts

264
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:22,880
and this loss has hit me kind of hard.

265
00:13:22,880 --> 00:13:24,360
Well, Pete, you know

266
00:13:24,360 --> 00:13:27,960
there's nothing about this that isn't upsetting,

267
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:31,080
And I've had the same sort of thing happen to me

268
00:13:31,080 --> 00:13:33,439
and don't feel alone, because I'm sure

269
00:13:33,439 --> 00:13:34,920
that other people listening

270
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:37,480
have had the same kind of thing happen to them.

271
00:13:37,480 --> 00:13:39,640
So let me offer you this:

272
00:13:39,640 --> 00:13:43,480
Either you were the cause of the scouts changing troops

273
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:44,760
or you weren't.

274
00:13:44,760 --> 00:13:46,480
Now, if you were the cause,

275
00:13:46,480 --> 00:13:49,280
that's kind of the worst case scenario.

276
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:50,640
Well, you know what?

277
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:53,120
That's just the way of things sometimes.

278
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:55,600
Sometimes people don't get along with each other,

279
00:13:55,600 --> 00:13:59,000
sometimes people don't like somebody's leadership style

280
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,720
or personality or something like that.

281
00:14:01,720 --> 00:14:04,160
I've had it happen to me.

282
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:05,000
like I said,

283
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,480
people who are listening have had it happen to them

284
00:14:07,480 --> 00:14:09,480
and that's little consolation, I know,

285
00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:11,400
because this is very upsetting,

286
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:14,199
but it's not the end of the world-

287
00:14:14,199 --> 00:14:17,600
If you weren't the cause of these scouts leaving-

288
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:19,840
because- let's note this-

289
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:22,560
the first scout that went said he wanted to be with his friend,

290
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:25,400
and probably the second scout decided the same thing,

291
00:14:25,400 --> 00:14:28,640
and that's a pretty common reason for changing troops,

292
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:32,760
because boys want to be scouts with their buddies

293
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:35,680
and if their buddies are all over in another troop,

294
00:14:35,680 --> 00:14:37,520
it would seem very natural to me

295
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:39,600
that they would want to go over to the other troop.

296
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:42,000
It's a very common reason for changing troops

297
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,280
and I would accept that at face value.

298
00:14:44,280 --> 00:14:45,880
and it is what it is,

299
00:14:45,880 --> 00:14:47,920
And you aren't going to be able to do anything

300
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:51,200
to change that, because scouts pick their friends.

301
00:14:51,200 --> 00:14:54,160
Don't take it as a personal affront to your dedication

302
00:14:54,160 --> 00:14:56,360
or the resources you put into scouting.

303
00:14:56,360 --> 00:14:59,200
Listen, the boys that we serve as scouts

304
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:01,680
are at an age where they can be very capricious

305
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:04,240
and thoughtless when it comes to things like this.

306
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:05,600
Now, you'd hope that their parents,

307
00:15:05,600 --> 00:15:08,120
especially those who are volunteers,

308
00:15:08,120 --> 00:15:09,680
would be a little more thoughtful,

309
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:11,160
but sometimes they aren't.

310
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:13,920
So, Pete, this is where your sense of professionalism

311
00:15:13,920 --> 00:15:16,780
about your work as a scoutmaster needs to kick in.

312
00:15:16,819 --> 00:15:19,900
It's easy to be resentful or to take this personally,

313
00:15:19,900 --> 00:15:21,180
but as a scoutmaster,

314
00:15:21,180 --> 00:15:23,579
you're expected to have a higher standard

315
00:15:23,579 --> 00:15:25,300
in the way you approach this,

316
00:15:25,300 --> 00:15:28,060
even if other people act poorly.

317
00:15:28,060 --> 00:15:30,699
So you focus on the scouts who are there

318
00:15:30,699 --> 00:15:33,220
and you'll get over the ones that you lost.

319
00:15:33,220 --> 00:15:36,939
And I would add that losing scouts one way or another

320
00:15:36,939 --> 00:15:39,620
is both inevitable and expected.

321
00:15:39,620 --> 00:15:40,900
It always hurts a little bit

322
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:42,740
and sometimes it hurts a lot,

323
00:15:42,740 --> 00:15:45,420
but scoutmasters have important work to do

324
00:15:45,420 --> 00:15:48,100
and we need to have a sense of professionalism

325
00:15:48,100 --> 00:15:49,340
about that work.

326
00:15:49,340 --> 00:15:52,140
And I hasten to add that this is also an opportunity

327
00:15:52,140 --> 00:15:54,260
to do a little introspection:

328
00:15:54,260 --> 00:15:55,580
take a look at your program,

329
00:15:55,580 --> 00:15:56,940
take a look at your attitudes

330
00:15:56,940 --> 00:15:58,740
and the way that you're approaching things

331
00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:00,780
and see if there's any room for improvement.

332
00:16:00,780 --> 00:16:02,020
I know when I do that,

333
00:16:02,020 --> 00:16:04,660
I always find room for improvement.

334
00:16:04,660 --> 00:16:06,980
Hey, listen, if you have a question or a comment

335
